14	/**
15	 * <p>The <b>panel-on-parchment</b> and its arithmetic: the frame, the title, the rule, the clamp that
16	 * keeps it from running off the top, the rows that stack up, and the {@code EditBox}es with their label
17	 * above. Nothing else. It doesn't decide what buttons go at the bottom or when anything gets sent.</p>
18	 *
19	 * <p>It exists because two screens were writing this same thing separately. {@link SmallFormScreen} is a
20	 * form: it gets filled in and <i>Confirm</i> is pressed once. {@link SheetAdjustScreen} is a control
21	 * panel: ten independent actions that each apply on their own, with no <i>Confirm</i> at all. They're
22	 * two different contracts, which is why the latter could never extend the former — but the frame they
23	 * draw is the same, and that part really was duplicated: {@code parseIntOr}, {@code cycleLabel}, the
24	 * {@code onClose} that returns to the parent, the fields' {@code tick}, the {@code Math.max(44, ...)},
25	 * and the panel's 14px padding were all written twice.</p>
26	 *
27	 * <p>The detail that gave it away: {@code SmallFormScreen} carried the comment <i>"Same clamp as
28	 * SheetAdjustScreen"</i>. The base class had copied the number from the screen that went its own way,
29	 * not the other way around. With this, the number lives in one place.</p>
30	 */
